blo sbyong mtshon cha'i 'khor lo|(Text) Author: Dharmarakshita. Question: Does it mean "The Whell with Sharp Weapons"? ALEX: NO, IT LOOKS LIKE A THROWING STAR WEAPON THAT SAMURAI OR NINJAS USE. LIKE A WAGON WHEEL, WITHOUT THE OUSIDE RIM, AND THE SPOKES ARE MADE OF KNIVES. Question: Is “The Wheel of Sharp Weapons” some kind of samurai weapon or does it only look like weapon? If the first, does it have some traditional name, which can be found in Wikipedia? If the latter, then what is it? ALEX: IT IS THE WEAPON. THE JAPANESE NAME FOR IT IS SHURIKEN, BUT PLEASE DON'T TRANSLATE IT THAT WAY, SINCE NO ONE WILL KNOW WHAT IT IS, http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuriken THE WHEEL OF SHARP WEAPONS WAS JUST THE TITLE THAT I MADE UP WHEN I FIRST TRANSLATED THE TEXT IN THE 1970'S. LITERALLY, THE WORD IN THE TITLE MEANS "WEAPON-WHEEL" OR "WEAPON-CIRCLE." IN MY NEW TRANSLATION, I MADE THE TRANSLATION OF THE WORD MORE PRECISE, AS THROWING STAR WEAPON. WHAT DO YOU SUGGEST IN RUSSIAN? Question: Why has Dharmarakshita called hit text “The Wheel of Sharp Weapons”? What is the meaning of the name of the text in terms of it’s content? ALEX: THE THROWING STAR WEAPON IS LIKE AN AUSTRALIAN BOOMERANG. YOU THROW IT OUT AND IT COMES BACK TO YOU. LIKE SUCH A WEAPON, THE NEGATIVE KARMA THAT WE CREATE, FROM DESTRUCTIVE BEHAVIOR THAT WE THROW OUT TO OTHERS, CIRCLES BACK TO US AND CAUSES SUFFERING TO US Question: Is it possible to call shuriken "the weapon with sharp blades"? ALEX: HOW ABOUT WHEEL WEAPON WITH SHARP BLADES. THE LITERAL TRANSLATION IS WHEEL OF SHARP WEAPONS. Question: Concerning “Wheel Weapon With Sharp Blades”, does the word “Wheel” mean “round”, “circular” here? ALEX: YES. IT LOOKS LIKE A STAR “The Round Weapon with Sharp Blades” is not very good variant, because Russian word for “round” can also mean “spherical”. We can’t say “The Wheel Weapon”, because we need to say “The weapon in the form of a wheel”. We can’t say “The Wheel of Sharp Weapons”, because we can’t use genitive in this situation (it is impossible to say “the wheel of what? – the wheel of weapons”). We have two variants. One is “The Disc with Sharp Blades” – without “weapon”. The other is “The Weapon with Sharp Blades” – without “round”. ALEX: DISC WITH SHARP BLADES.
