mi bslu ba|Not incorrect.
mi dge ba|States of mind, or physical, verbal, or mental actions motivated by them, which ripen into unhappiness or the suffering of problems or pain, to be experienced by the person on whose mental continuum they occur. Since the term carries no connotation of moral judgment, the translation "nonvirtuous" is misleading for this term.
mi g.yo ba|(Iconographic)
mi gnas pa'i mya ngan 'das|(1) According to the Hinayana tenet systems, the static unchanging state of full enlightenment attained by a Buddha and lasting only so long as he is alive. In this state, a Buddha does not abide in either the extreme of continued samsaric suffering or in the extreme of the passivity of a Hinayana arhat's nirvana without residue. (2) According to the Mahayana tenet systems, including Gelug Prasangika, the static unchanging state of full enlightenment attained by a Buddha and lasting forever, in which a Buddha does not abide in either the extreme of continued samsaric suffering or in the extreme of the passivity of a Hinayana arhat's nirvana without residue.
mi la ras pa bzhad pa rdo rje|(Person, 1040–1123)
mi nag gdung gdung|(Person)
mi nyag|(Dynasty) Tibetanized kingdom in Kham.
mi nyag|(Geographic)
