padma dkar po|(Person)
pan chen bla ma|(Other)
pan chen blo bzang chos kyi rgyal mtshan|(Person)
pan chen bsod nams grags pa|(Person)
pan chen chos kyi rgyal mtshan 'phrin las lhun grub|(Person)
pan chen dpal ldan ye shes|(Person)
pan chen sha kya shri grva tshang|(Monastery)
pha bong kha byams pa bstan 'dzin 'phrin las rgya mtsho|(Person)
pha dam pa sangs rgyas|(Person)
pha ra khang tsan|(Monastery)
pha rol tu phyin pa|A mental factor that brings one to the far shore of samsara, either to liberation or to enlightenment. There are either six or ten far-reaching attitudes. Also called "perfection." Theravada and Mahayana give slightly different lists of these. According to Mahayana, the six are the far-reaching attitudes of (1) generosity, (2) ethical self-discipline, (3) patience, (4) joyful perseverance, (5) mental stability, and (6) discriminating awareness (wisdom). The Mahayana list of ten adds the far-reaching attitudes of (7) skill in means, (8) aspirational prayer, (9) strengthening, and (10) deep awareness.
phag gru'i gdan sa thel|(Monastery) the earliest Kagyu monastery.
phag mo gru pa|(Dynasty)
phag mo gru pa|(Person)
phag mo gru pa rdo rje rgyal po|(Person, 1110 – 1170) highly educated monk from Kham, disciple of the Kagyu master Gampopa.
phag mo gru pa rdo rje rgyal po|(Person, 1110-1170) Also known as Dorjey-gyelpo. A highly educated monk from Kham, disciple of the Kagyu master Gampopa.
'phags bstod|(Text)
'phags lam|The three pathway minds of shravaka, pratyekabuddha, and bodhisattva aryas (those with nonconceptual cognition of the four noble truths) -- namely, their seeing and accustoming pathway minds, and their pathway minds needing no further training.
'phags pa|A practitioner who has had nonconceptual cognition of the four noble truths and thus has attained a shravaka, pratyekabuddha, or bodhisattva seeing pathway of mind (path of seeing). Also called a "highly realized practitioner" or a "highly realized being." Some translators render the term as "noble one."
'phags pa chos rgyal|(Person)
'phags pa dam pa'i chos dran pa nye bar gzhag pa|(Text) Skt: Full: Āryasaddharmasmṛtyupasthāna Short:.
'phags pa lha|(Person)
'phags pa ma skyes dgra'i 'gyod pa bsal ba'i mdo|(Text) Skt: Full: Short: Aryajatashatru-kaukrtyavinodana Sutra.
'phags pa nam mkha' mdzod kyis zhus pa'i mdo|(Text) Skt: Full: Short: Arya-gaganagaganja-pariprccha Sutra.
'phags pa phung po gsum pa zhes bya ba theg pa chen po'i mdo|(Text) Skt: Full: Short: Arya-triskandhaka-nama-mahayana-sutra.
'phags pa rgyal bu don grub kyi mdo|(Text) Skt: Full: Short: Aryajinaputra-arthasiddhi Sutra.
'phags pa rnam par 'thag pa thams cad bsdus pa zhes bya ba theg pa chen po'i mdo|(Text) Skt: with Diacritics: Full: Sarva-vaidalya-saṃgraha Sūtra Short:.
'phags pa shes rab kyi pha rol tu phyin ma bsdus pa'i tshig le'ur bya ba|(Text) Author: Dignaga. Skt: Full: Ārya-prajñāpāramitā-saṃgraha-kārikā Short:.
'phags pa shes rab kyi pha rol tu phyin pa brgyad stong pa'i dka' 'grel snying po mchog|(Text) Author: Ratnakarashanti (Shantipa) (shanti pa).
'phags pa yongs su mya ngan las 'das pa chen po'i mdo|(Text) Skt: Full: Āryamahāparinirvāṇa Sūtra Short:.
phan sems|The mental factor of wishing to benefit others, wishing others well. Often translated by others as "altruism."
'phangs pa'i 'bras bu'i yan lag|In the twelve links of dependent arising, the three and a half links of resultant loaded consciousness, nameable mental faculties with or without gross form, stimulators of cognition, and contacting awareness, which occur during the development of an embryo in the womb in the life that is thrown by the activated karmic aftermath of throwing karma.
phar phyin|A mental factor that brings one to the far shore of samsara, either to liberation or to enlightenment. There are either six or ten far-reaching attitudes. Also called "perfection" or, in Sanskrit, "paramita."
phar phyin bsdus pa|(Text) Author: Aryashura. Skt: with Diacritics: Full: Pāramitā-samāsa Short:.
'phen byed kyi yan lag|In the twelve links of dependent arising, the two and a half links of unawareness, affecting impulses, and causal loaded consciousness, which describe the process through which throwing karma is built up and its karmic aftermath is "planted" on a mental continuum as the cause for a next rebirth.
